1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:04,230
In the year 2000, a small startup
walked into Blockbuster's headquarters

2
00:00:04,280 --> 00:00:05,390
with an offer.

3
00:00:05,440 --> 00:00:08,150
Buy us for $50 million.

4
00:00:08,200 --> 00:00:10,990
Blockbuster's executives listened...

5
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:12,790
and said no.

6
00:00:12,840 --> 00:00:16,270
Ten years later, that
startup was worth billions.

7
00:00:16,320 --> 00:00:17,590
And Blockbuster?

8
00:00:17,640 --> 00:00:18,990
Bankrupt.

9
00:00:19,040 --> 00:00:23,030
This is the story of the most
expensive "no" in business history.

10
00:00:23,080 --> 00:00:26,750
Blockbuster began in
1985 in Dallas, Texas.

11
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:32,430
The idea was simple: big, bright stores,
thousands of movies, and a guarantee

12
00:00:32,480 --> 00:00:35,150
you'd find what you
wanted on a Friday night.

13
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:36,750
It worked.

14
00:00:36,800 --> 00:00:40,830
At its peak, in the mid-2000s,
Blockbuster operated roughly nine

15
00:00:40,880 --> 00:00:46,030
thousand stores worldwide and
employed tens of thousands of people.

16
00:00:46,080 --> 00:00:49,870
Friday night at Blockbuster was
a ritual for a whole generation.

17
00:00:49,920 --> 00:00:51,790
But there was a hidden problem.

18
00:00:51,840 --> 00:00:55,670
A huge slice of the company's profit
didn't come from renting movies.

19
00:00:55,720 --> 00:00:57,910
It came from late fees.

20
00:00:57,960 --> 00:01:03,960
Every forgotten tape, every overdue
DVD, quietly fed the machine.

21
00:01:04,080 --> 00:01:08,850
Meanwhile, in 1997, Reed Hastings
and Marc Randolph had started

22
00:01:08,900 --> 00:01:09,790
Netflix.

23
00:01:09,840 --> 00:01:14,270
Their model: rent DVDs online
and deliver them by mail.

24
00:01:14,320 --> 00:01:15,350
No store.

25
00:01:15,400 --> 00:01:17,110
No late fees.

26
00:01:17,160 --> 00:01:21,950
By the year 2000, Netflix was
losing money and needed a lifeline.

27
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:26,170
Hastings and his team met with Blockbuster's
leadership and proposed a deal:

28
00:01:26,220 --> 00:01:30,670
Blockbuster would buy Netflix for
about $50 million, and Netflix would

29
00:01:30,720 --> 00:01:33,390
run Blockbuster's online business.

30
00:01:33,440 --> 00:01:34,710
Blockbuster passed.

31
00:01:34,760 --> 00:01:38,890
According to accounts from people in the
room, the idea wasn't taken seriously.

32
00:01:38,940 --> 00:01:41,590
Online rental looked like a niche.

33
00:01:41,640 --> 00:01:44,390
Blockbuster had thousands of stores.

34
00:01:44,440 --> 00:01:47,810
Why bother with a tiny company
shipping discs in red envelopes?

35
00:01:47,860 --> 00:01:50,030
Netflix kept growing.

36
00:01:50,080 --> 00:01:53,550
By 2004, it had millions of subscribers.

37
00:01:53,600 --> 00:01:57,150
Blockbuster finally woke up, and launched
its own online service and a program

38
00:01:57,200 --> 00:01:58,990
called Total Access.

39
00:01:59,040 --> 00:02:00,630
It was aggressive.

40
00:02:00,680 --> 00:02:02,310
It was good.

41
00:02:02,360 --> 00:02:05,550
And in 2004, the company
also took a bold step:

42
00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:08,070
it scrapped late fees in its stores.

43
00:02:08,120 --> 00:02:09,910
But there was a catch.

44
00:02:09,960 --> 00:02:13,830
The change cut into revenue that Wall
Street had come to depend on, and the

45
00:02:13,880 --> 00:02:16,910
company was also carrying heavy debt.

46
00:02:16,960 --> 00:02:19,570
Investor Carl Icahn pushed back hard.

47
00:02:19,620 --> 00:02:24,950
CEO John Antioco, who had championed the
new strategy, clashed with the board

48
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:30,070
over how much to spend,
and in 2007, he left.

49
00:02:30,120 --> 00:02:33,430
His replacement pulled back on the
online push and went back to focusing on

50
00:02:33,480 --> 00:02:34,710
stores.

51
00:02:34,760 --> 00:02:39,070
By then, Netflix had already started
streaming movies over the internet.

52
00:02:39,120 --> 00:02:40,950
The timing was brutal.

53
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:42,870
The financial crisis hit.

54
00:02:42,920 --> 00:02:44,250
Stores were closing.

55
00:02:44,300 --> 00:02:47,380
Redbox kiosks were renting
movies for a dollar.

56
00:02:47,430 --> 00:02:53,390
And Netflix was now in living rooms,
on game consoles, and on phones.

57
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:58,230
In September 2010, Blockbuster
filed for bankruptcy.

58
00:02:58,280 --> 00:03:02,230
In 2011, Dish Network bought its
remaining assets for roughly

59
00:03:02,280 --> 00:03:04,170
$300 million.

60
00:03:04,220 --> 00:03:09,470
Today, only one Blockbuster store remains
open, in Bend, Oregon, and it has

61
00:03:09,520 --> 00:03:11,710
become a tourist attraction.

62
00:03:11,760 --> 00:03:13,950
So what actually went wrong?

63
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:15,310
Three things.

64
00:03:15,360 --> 00:03:16,750
One.

65
00:03:16,800 --> 00:03:20,030
They defended the old model
instead of building the new one.

66
00:03:20,080 --> 00:03:24,070
Late fees and stores felt safe, so the
company protected them while customers

67
00:03:24,120 --> 00:03:25,630
moved on.

68
00:03:25,680 --> 00:03:26,710
Two.

69
00:03:26,760 --> 00:03:29,950
They mistook a small
competitor for a harmless one.

70
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:32,310
Netflix looked tiny.

71
00:03:32,360 --> 00:03:35,950
But it was growing exactly
where customers were heading.

72
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:37,310
Three.

73
00:03:37,360 --> 00:03:40,750
They changed too late, and
without full commitment.

74
00:03:40,800 --> 00:03:45,510
When Blockbuster finally moved, leadership
fought itself, and the strategy was

75
00:03:45,560 --> 00:03:47,430
never given a real chance.

76
00:03:47,480 --> 00:03:52,030
Netflix, on the other hand, made
a habit of cannibalizing itself.

77
00:03:52,080 --> 00:03:57,390
It moved from DVDs to streaming, then
from streaming to making its own shows.

78
00:03:57,440 --> 00:04:00,910
It chose to disrupt itself
before someone else did.

79
00:04:00,960 --> 00:04:03,470
Blockbuster didn't die
because it was stupid.

80
00:04:03,520 --> 00:04:05,950
It died because it was comfortable.

81
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:08,750
The question every business
has to ask is simple:

82
00:04:08,800 --> 00:04:11,270
are we protecting what
made us successful...

83
00:04:11,320 --> 00:04:13,550
or building what comes next?

84
00:04:13,600 --> 00:04:16,350
If you enjoyed this story, subscribe.

85
00:04:16,400 --> 00:04:20,430
Next up: the company that
invented the digital camera...

86
00:04:20,480 --> 00:04:21,780
and still went bankrupt.
